WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:04.259
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.259 --> 00:00:09.840
I think this is one of the more important
things that happened in AI research lately.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:15.450
In the last few years, we have seen DeepMind
defeat the best Go players in the world, and

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:22.280
after OpenAI’s venture in the game of DOTA2,
it’s time for DeepMind to shine again as

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:26.160
they take on Starcraft 2, a real-time strategy
game.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:31.609
The depth and the amount of skill required
to play this game is simply astounding.

00:00:31.609 --> 00:00:38.300
The search space of Starcraft 2 is so vast
that it exceeds both Chess, and even Go by

00:00:38.300 --> 00:00:40.010
a significant margin.

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:45.420
Also, it is a game that requires a great deal
of mechanical skill, split-second decision

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:51.960
making and we have imperfect information as
we only see what our units can see.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:55.329
A nightmare situation for any AI.

00:00:55.329 --> 00:01:01.420
DeepMind invited a beloved pro player, TLO
to play a few games against their new StarCraft

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:05.590
2 AI that goes by the name AlphaStar.

00:01:05.590 --> 00:01:12.520
Note that TLO a profesional player who is
easily in top 1% of players, or even better.

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:15.609
Mid-grandmaster for those who play StarCraft
2.

00:01:15.609 --> 00:01:20.849
This video is about what happened during this
event, and later, I will make another video

00:01:20.849 --> 00:01:24.939
that describes the algorithm that was used
to create this AI.

00:01:24.939 --> 00:01:29.240
The paper is still under review, so it will
take a little time until I can get my hands

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:30.240
on it.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:34.639
At the end of this video, you will also see
the inner workings of this AI.

00:01:34.639 --> 00:01:36.259
Let’s dive in.

00:01:36.259 --> 00:01:42.680
This is an AI that looked at a few games played
by human players, and after that initial step,

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:47.389
it learns by playing against itself for about
200 years.

00:01:47.389 --> 00:01:52.020
In our next episode, you will see how this
is even possible, so I hope you are subscribed

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:53.279
to the series.

00:01:53.279 --> 00:01:59.859
You see here that the AI controls the blue
units, and TLO, the human player plays red.

00:01:59.859 --> 00:02:03.799
Right at the start of the first game, the
AI did something interesting.

00:02:03.799 --> 00:02:07.630
In fact, what is interesting is what it didn’t
do.

00:02:07.630 --> 00:02:12.849
It started to create new buildings next to
its nexus, instead of building a walloff that

00:02:12.849 --> 00:02:14.379
you can see here.

00:02:14.379 --> 00:02:19.599
Using a walloff is considered standard practice
in most games, and the AI used these buildings

00:02:19.599 --> 00:02:24.989
to not wall off the entrance, but to shield
away the workers from possible attacks.

00:02:24.989 --> 00:02:29.770
Now note that this is not unheard of, but
this is also not a strategy that is widely

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:33.379
played today and is considered non-standard.

00:02:33.379 --> 00:02:38.290
It also built more worker units than what
is universally accepted as standard, we found

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:44.219
out later that this was partly done in anticipation
of losing a few of them early on.

00:02:44.219 --> 00:02:45.219
Very cool.

00:02:45.219 --> 00:02:51.439
Then, almost before we even knew what happened,
it won the first game a little more than 7

00:02:51.439 --> 00:03:07.550
minutes in, which is very quick, noting that
in-game time is a little faster than real-time.

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:13.459
The thought process of TLO at this point is
that that’s interesting, but okay, well,

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:17.260
the AI plays aggressively and managed to pull
this one off.

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:18.260
No big deal.

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:23.439
We will fire up the second game, in the meantime,
few interesting details.

00:03:23.439 --> 00:03:28.660
The goal of setting up the details of this
algorithm was that the number of actions performed

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:35.969
by the AI roughly matches a human player,
and hopefully it still plays as well, or better.

00:03:35.969 --> 00:03:38.959
It has to make meaningful strategic decisions.

00:03:38.959 --> 00:03:43.609
You see here that this checks out for the
average actions every minute, but if you look

00:03:43.609 --> 00:03:48.519
here, you see around the tail end here that
there are times when it performs more actions

00:03:48.519 --> 00:03:54.420
than humans and this may enable playstyles
that are not accessible for human players.

00:03:54.420 --> 00:04:00.639
However, note that many times it also does
miraculous things with very few actions.

00:04:00.639 --> 00:04:04.790
Now, what about an other important detail,
reaction time?

00:04:04.790 --> 00:04:10.190
The reaction time of the AI is set to 350ms,
which is quite slow.

00:04:10.190 --> 00:04:15.769
That’s excellent news because this is usually
a common angle of criticism for game AIs.

00:04:15.769 --> 00:04:21.310
The AI also sees the whole map at once, but
it is not given more information than what

00:04:21.310 --> 00:04:23.000
its units can see.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:27.530
This perhaps is the most commonly misunderstood
detail, so it is worth noting.

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:32.960
So, in other words, it sees exactly what a
human would see if the human would move the

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:38.890
camera around very quickly, but, it doesn’t
have to move the camera, which adds additional

00:04:38.890 --> 00:04:44.810
actions and cognitive load to the human, so
one might say that the AI has an edge here.

00:04:44.810 --> 00:04:50.240
The AI plays these games independently, what’s
more, each game was played by a different

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:55.430
AI, which also means that they do not memorize
what happened in the last game like a human

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:56.669
would.

00:04:56.669 --> 00:05:01.010
Early in the next game, we can see the utility
of the walloff in action which is able to

00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:04.460
completely prevent the AIs early attack.

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:09.530
Later that game, the AI used disruptors, a
unit, which if controlled with such level

00:05:09.530 --> 00:05:15.370
of expertise, can decimate the army of the
opponent with area damage by killing multiple

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:17.000
units at once.

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:21.270
It has done an outstanding job picking away
at the army of TLO.

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:27.860
Then, after getting a significant advantage,
AlphaStar loses it with a few sloppy plays

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:32.990
and by deciding to engage aggressively while
standing in tight choke points.

00:05:32.990 --> 00:05:35.690
You can see that this is not such a great
idea.

00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:41.750
This was quite surprising as this is considered
to be StarCraft 101 knowledge right there.

00:05:41.750 --> 00:05:46.930
During the remainder of the match, the commentators
mentioned that they play and watch matches

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:52.300
all the time and the AI came up with an army
composition that they have never seen during

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:53.690
a professional match.

00:05:53.690 --> 00:05:56.940
And, the AI won this one too.

00:05:56.940 --> 00:06:01.860
After this game it became clear that these
agents can play any style in the game.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:03.510
Which is terrifying.

00:06:03.510 --> 00:06:08.599
Here you can see an alternative visualization
that shows a little more of the inner workings

00:06:08.599 --> 00:06:10.409
of the neural network.

00:06:10.409 --> 00:06:14.949
We can see what information it gets from the
game, a visualization of neurons that get

00:06:14.949 --> 00:06:21.030
activated within the network, what locations
and units are considered for the next actions,

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:26.639
and whether the AI predicts itself as the
winner or loser of the game.

00:06:26.639 --> 00:06:31.210
If you look carefully, you will also see the
moment when the agent becomes certain that

00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:32.349
it will win this game.

00:06:32.349 --> 00:06:37.370
I could look at this all day long, and if
you feel the same way, make sure to visit

00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:41.180
the video description, I have a link to the
source video for you.

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:57.870
The final result against TLO was 5 to 0, so
that’s something, and he mentioned that

00:06:57.870 --> 00:07:03.370
the AlphaStar played very much like a human
does and almost always managed to outmaneuver

00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:04.370
him.

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:09.270
However, TLO also mentioned that he is confident
that upon playing more training matches against

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:12.460
these agents, he would be able to defeat the
AI.

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:14.789
I hope he will be given a chance to do that.

00:07:14.789 --> 00:07:18.569
This AI seems strong, but still beatable.

00:07:18.569 --> 00:07:23.039
I would also note that many of you would probably
expect the later versions of AlphaStar to

00:07:23.039 --> 00:07:25.360
be way better than this one.

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:30.080
The good news is that the story continues
and we’ll see whether that’s true!

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:34.220
So at this point, the DeepMind scientists
said that “maybe we could try to be a bit

00:07:34.220 --> 00:07:38.669
more ambitious”, and asked “can you bring
us someone better”?

00:07:38.669 --> 00:07:43.060
And in the meantime, pressed that training
button on the AI again.

00:07:43.060 --> 00:07:46.620
In comes MaNa, a top tier pro player.

00:07:46.620 --> 00:07:49.449
One of the best Protoss players in the world.

00:07:49.449 --> 00:07:54.229
This was a nerve-wracking moment for DeepMind
scientists as well, because their agents played

00:07:54.229 --> 00:08:00.020
against each other, so they only knew the
AI’s winrate against a different AI, but

00:08:00.020 --> 00:08:04.099
they didn’t know how they would compete
against a top pro player.

00:08:04.099 --> 00:08:06.610
It may still have holes in its strategy.

00:08:06.610 --> 00:08:08.580
Who knows what would happen?

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:12.350
Understandably, they had very little confidence
in winning this one.

00:08:12.350 --> 00:08:17.639
What they didn’t expect is that this new
AI was not slightly improved, or somewhat

00:08:17.639 --> 00:08:18.639
improved.

00:08:18.639 --> 00:08:20.080
No, no, no.

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:23.170
This new AI was next level.

00:08:23.170 --> 00:08:29.090
This set of improved agents among many other
skills, had incredibly crisp micromanagement

00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:31.220
of each individual unit.

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:35.340
In the first game, we’ve seen it pulling
back injured units but still letting them

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:41.420
attack from afar masterfully, leading to an
early win for the AI against Mana in the first

00:08:41.420 --> 00:08:42.420
game.

00:08:42.420 --> 00:08:46.490
He and the commentators were equally shocked
by how well the agent played.

00:08:46.490 --> 00:08:51.500
And I will add that I remember from watching
many games from a now inactive player by the

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:54.470
name MarineKing a few years ago.

00:08:54.470 --> 00:08:59.580
And I vividly remember that he played some
of his games so well, the commentators said

00:08:59.580 --> 00:09:03.240
that there’s no better way to put it, he
played like a god.

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:08.960
I am almost afraid to say that this micromanagement
was even more crisp than that.

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:11.820
This AI plays phenomenal games.

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:17.090
In later matches, the AI did things that seemed
like blunders, like attacking on ramps and

00:09:17.090 --> 00:09:23.270
standing in choke points, or using unfavorable
unit compositions and refusing to change it

00:09:23.270 --> 00:09:29.510
and, get this, it still won all of those games
5 to 0.

00:09:29.510 --> 00:09:31.810
Against a top pro player.

00:09:31.810 --> 00:09:53.870
Let that sink in.

00:09:53.870 --> 00:09:59.710
The competition was closed by a match where
the AI was asked to also do the camera management.

00:09:59.710 --> 00:10:05.450
The agent was still very competent, but somewhat
weaker and as a result, lost this game, hence

00:10:05.450 --> 00:10:08.990
the “0 or 1” part in the title.

00:10:08.990 --> 00:10:13.890
My impression is that it was asked to do something
that it was not designed for, and expect a

00:10:13.890 --> 00:10:17.210
future version to be able to handle this use
case as well.

00:10:17.210 --> 00:10:21.990
I will also commend Mana for his solid game
plan for this game, and also, huge respect

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:24.370
for DeepMind for their sportsmanship.

00:10:24.370 --> 00:10:29.630
Interestingly, in this match, Mana also started
using a worker oversaturation strategy that

00:10:29.630 --> 00:10:31.200
I mentioned earlier.

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:35.290
This he learned from AlphaStar and used it
in his winning game.

00:10:35.290 --> 00:10:37.279
Isn’t that amazing?

00:10:37.279 --> 00:10:42.710
DeepMind also offered a reddit AMA where anyone
could ask them questions to make sure to clear

00:10:42.710 --> 00:10:47.940
up any confusion, for instance, the actions
per minute part has been addressed, I’ve

00:10:47.940 --> 00:10:50.830
included a link to that for you in the description.

00:10:50.830 --> 00:10:56.340
To go from a turn-based perfect information
game, like Go, to a real time strategy game

00:10:56.340 --> 00:11:02.000
of imperfect information in about a year sounds
like science fiction to me.

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:03.990
And yet, here it is.

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:09.370
Also, note that DeepMind’s goal is not to
create a godlike StarCraft 2 AI.

00:11:09.370 --> 00:11:14.610
They want to solve intelligence, not StarCraft
2, and they used the game as a vehicle to

00:11:14.610 --> 00:11:19.740
demonstrate its long-term decision making
capabilities against human players.

00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:24.160
One more important thing to emphasize is that
the building blocks of AlphaStar are meant

00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:30.750
to be reasonably general AI algorithms, which
means that parts of this AI can be reused

00:11:30.750 --> 00:11:37.010
for other things, for instance, Demis Hassabis
mentioned weather prediction and climate modeling

00:11:37.010 --> 00:11:38.420
as examples.

00:11:38.420 --> 00:11:42.490
If you take only one thought from this video,
let it be this one.

00:11:42.490 --> 00:11:47.130
I urge you to watch all the matches because
what you are witnessing may very well be history

00:11:47.130 --> 00:11:48.210
in the making.

00:11:48.210 --> 00:11:53.910
I put a link to the whole event in the video
description, plus plenty more materials, including

00:11:53.910 --> 00:11:59.150
other people’s analysis, Mana’s personal
experience of the event, his breakdown of

00:11:59.150 --> 00:12:02.760
his games and what was going through his head
during the event.

00:12:02.760 --> 00:12:07.480
I highly recommend checking out his 5th game,
but really, go through them all, it’s a

00:12:07.480 --> 00:12:08.480
ton of fun!

00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:13.190
I made sure to include a more skeptical analysis
of the game as well to give you a balanced

00:12:13.190 --> 00:12:14.820
portfolio of insights.

00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:19.960
Also, huge respect for DeepMind and the players
who practiced their chops for many many years

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:23.380
and have played really well under immense
pressure.

00:12:23.380 --> 00:12:25.230
Thank you all for this delightful event.

00:12:25.230 --> 00:12:26.570
It really made my day.

00:12:26.570 --> 00:12:30.770
And the ultimate question is, how long did
it take to train these agents?

00:12:30.770 --> 00:12:32.410
2 weeks.

00:12:32.410 --> 00:12:33.410
Wow.

00:12:33.410 --> 00:12:38.070
And what’s more, after the training step,
the AI can be deployed on an inexpensive consumer

00:12:38.070 --> 00:12:39.450
desktop machine.

00:12:39.450 --> 00:12:41.910
And this is only the first version.

00:12:41.910 --> 00:12:47.250
This is just a taste, and it would be hard
to overstate how big of a milestone this is.

00:12:47.250 --> 00:12:52.250
And now, scientists at DeepMind have sufficient
data to calculate the amount of resources

00:12:52.250 --> 00:12:56.260
they need to spend to train the next, even
more improved agents.

00:12:56.260 --> 00:13:00.530
I am confident that they will also take into
consideration the feedback from the StarCraft

00:13:00.530 --> 00:13:03.420
community when creating this next version.

00:13:03.420 --> 00:13:05.240
What a time to be alive!

00:13:05.240 --> 00:13:06.980
What do you think about all this?

00:13:06.980 --> 00:13:07.980
Any predictions?

00:13:07.980 --> 00:13:10.410
Is this harder than DOTA2?

00:13:10.410 --> 00:13:12.380
Let me know in the comments section below.

00:13:12.380 --> 00:13:18.160
And remember, we humans build up new strategies
by learning from each other, and of course,

00:13:18.160 --> 00:13:22.000
the AI, as you have seen here, doesn’t care
about any of that.

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:26.860
It doesn’t need intuition and can come up
with unusual strategies.

00:13:26.860 --> 00:13:32.200
The difference now is that these strategies
work against some of the best human players.

00:13:32.200 --> 00:13:37.110
Now it’s time for us to finally start learning
from an AI. gg.

00:13:37.110 --> 00:13:39.810
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

